les is one of the  main drivers for brexit so talks will be  tough in my view there  EU has a symmetrically bigger power when  it comes to when it comes to trade each  country they you will be losing one  market the UK will be losing  seven markets there is one element where  I think the UK has got perhaps more of a  say and that is in the financial markets  and that is basically London the city  has served as a major financial hub for  the whole of the EU as the EU doesn't  want to lose that expertise the UK could  use the City of London as a bargaining  chip in the negotiations and then  there's the issue of fishing rights that  could be a stumbling stone on the bumpy  road to brexit taking back control of  British waters was a key brexit campaign  pledge but the UK may have to compromise  if it doesn't want the issue to scupper  chances of finalizing a deal with the EU  the UK leaving in my view was  unjustified and therefore I believe that  it's going to be at the economic cost  for the UK so the closer we can keep  when it comes down to the way we do  trade for example and the better is  gonna be for both of us it's going to be  an extremely tight race to get things  done by the end of the year I'm joined  by David McAllister brexit coordinator  for the European Parliament MEP mr.  McAllister where do you see the red  lines in the upcoming negotiations  well the negotiations are gonna start  presumably at the beginning of March  we're now awaiting for the Commissioner  to present the draft mandate then the  European Parliament will adopt the  resolution the MIL of February and then  finally the council will adopt the  guidelines on the 25th of February we're  under time pressure the UK government  has announced they were under no  circumstances want to prolong the  transition period that means the the  United Kingdom will definitely leave a  single mark and the customs union on the  31st of December that leaves us with  about 8 to 9 months to negotiate our  future relations we will have to  prioritize that's for sure we can't  cover all issues in this short time our  priorities are on the one hand trade  relations and on the other hand a  security cooperation  now the divorce deal was already  difficult enough how difficult would you  say or what do you expect from those  that the negotiations that are upcoming  will there be even more  pickled well of course negotiations are  always challenging and both interests  both sides have different interests but  the UK government and the European Union  have announced that they want to begin  these negotiations in a spirit of  goodwill mutual respect and  understanding both sides want to have a  solid foundation for our future  relations and Britain is leaving the  European Union but the UK will remain  our NATO ally our close political party  and many international organisations and  also the UK's an important trade partner  for us just as the single market is the  most important trade partner for the  United Kingdom so we all have a joint  interest that we should have solid  positive future relations but one thing  is clear a country which leaves the EU  than single market and the customs union  will no longer be in the same position  as a Member State now should a deal be  made by all means or would you say there  are concessions which the EU should not  make in negotiations in the end you must  always have the will to find a  compromise and the consensus and we have  always respected UK position but UK  positions have political consequences if  you voluntarily leave the single market  and the customs union you will have to  face with some of the consequences we  want to have as little friction in our  mutual trade as possible but the  precondition for this is a level playing  field we want a fair competition between  the UK and the European Union that's why  we will insist on a level playing field  our free principles are the integrity of  a single market the customs union c